schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: "Regenerative Finance (ReFi)"
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    Money flows that "build soil instead of mining it" — the article's core
    framing: rather than being extractive (treating a forest as worth more
    cut down than alive) or merely sustainable (doing less harm), ReFi aims
    to leave the system measurably healthier than it found it, using
    blockchain as a shared, tamper-resistant public record.
  source_traditions:
    - regenerative agriculture
    - blockchain / public ledgers
    - quadratic funding / mechanism design
    - carbon-market tokenization
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Introductory foundations-layer concept for community organizers and
    growers new to crypto; explicitly distinguishes "sustainability
    maintains, regeneration improves" and walks through three concrete
    problem/solution pairs (proving impact via Silvi, community-driven
    funding via Gitcoin quadratic funding, local value circulation via
    community tokens) before addressing energy-use and greenwashing
    objections.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - "sustainability (doing less harm — explicitly distinguished as a lesser goal than regeneration)"
    - "DeFi (decentralized finance generally, of which ReFi is framed as a values-driven subset)"
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      sustainability-maintains-vs-regeneration-improves — the article's
      central conceptual hinge distinguishing ReFi from ordinary
      sustainability framing.
    - >-
      tokenizing-is-not-automatically-good — a carbon token (or any
      tokenized "nature asset") is only as honest as the underlying project;
      tokenization is a wrapper, not a verification of quality.
  tensions:
    - >-
      pricing-nature-vs-commodifying-nature — putting a number on carbon,
      water, or biodiversity is presented as ReFi's headline move, but the
      article itself flags this is "exactly where greenwashing hides."
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Reducing ReFi to "carbon tokens" or "crypto for climate" erases the
    article's broader claim that it is fundamentally about redirecting money
    toward people doing regenerative work (soil, community, local
    circulation), not only about pricing ecological assets.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-is-refi.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
